  "query_preview": "..Current satellite and upper-air analysis show a \nshortwave trough over northern Arizona. This feature is \ninteracting with modest low-level moisture (surface dew points \nbetween 45 and 55 degrees F) to produce scattered showers, \nalthough a few isolated thunderstorms are possible this afternoon \nas well. Atmospheric instability is limited (CAPE of 100-500 \nJ/kg), but 0-6km bulk shear is elevated (25-35 kts) due to the \npassing shortwave trough. The best combination of instability and \nshear is expected along and east of the I-17, resulting in the \nhighest potential for showers and storms there. Today's storms \nwill be capable of producing outflow winds between 25-35 mph with \nisolated pockets of rainfall between 0.5-1.0 inches. While the \nprobability is low, a severe storm or two capable of producing 1 \ninch hail cannot be ruled out. \n\nOn Tuesday, a low pressure system is forecast to dive southward \nalong the West Coast. This will sandwich northern Arizona between \nthe deepening West Coast trough and broad high pressure over the \ncentral United States. As this happens, only minor changes to the \nsensible weather are expected. High temperatures are forecast to \nincrease by a couple of degrees with afternoon winds becoming \nmore breezy. High-resolution model guidance does indicates a low \npotential for some isolated shower, or thunderstorm activity, but \nstable conditions due to the presence of an inversion above the \nboundary layer should keep activity subdued. \n\nOn Wednesday and Thursday, the West Coast low shifts eastward\ntoward northern Arizona allowing deep subtropical moisture to push\ninto the area from the south. Some of this incoming moisture will\nbe assocaited with what is currently Tropical Storm Trudy \ncentered south of the Baja Peninsula. Model forecasts shows \nPrecipitable water (a measure of atmospheric moisture) ranging \nfrom 0.70 to 1.0 inches. This level of moisture is exceedingly \nrare for mid-October, with values above the 98th percentile. Due \nto this, flooding/flash Flooding will be the primary hazards. At \nthis point, the heaviest rainfall is expected along and south of \nthe Mogollon Rim, where the forecast calls for a broad area of \nstorm total rainfall of 1.0-3.0 inches. Further north over the \nNavajo Nation, storm total rainfall of 1.0-2.0 inches is forecast.\nThe severe weather potential is more difficult to assess, as \nthick cloud cover may limit instability. That said, forcing from \nthe passing low and strong wind shear (50+ kts) suggest at least a\nlow potential for severe, rotating storms. Otherwise, southerly \nwind speeds will increase during this time, strongest on Thursday.\nHowever, the probability of wind speeds exceeding wind advisory \nthresholds (>40 mph) is low. \n\nOn Friday, the forecast calls for decreasing chances for showers\nand thunderstorms through the day, and well below normal\ntemperatures. Forecast confidence in the evolution of the overall\nweather pattern heading into the weekend remains low.",
